Can you provide instructions on how to create my own HTML form using HTML, CSS and JavaScript?assistant

Sure! Here's an example of creating an HTML form:

Step 1: Create an HTML file
Create a new empty file and save it as `index.html`.

```
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
   <meta charset="UTF-8">
   <title>Form Example</title>
   <style>
      /* Add your CSS styles here */
   </style>
</head>
<body>
   <form action="#submit_form" method="POST"> 
      <!-- Add input fields below -->

      <label for="name">Name:</label>
      <input type="text" name="name" id="name"><br>

      <label for="email">Email Address:</label>
      <input type="email" name="email" id="email"><br>

      <label for="phone_number">Phone Number:</label>
      <input type="tel" name="phone_number" id="phone_number"><br><br>

      <button type="submit">Submit</button> 

      <!-- Add validation message container below -->

      <p id="error_message"></p>
   </form>

   <script>
      /* Add your JavaScript code here */
   </script>
</body>
</html>
```

In this basic form example, we've added three input fields (name, email, phone), a submit button and a container element (`<p>` tag) for displaying error messages.

Step 2: Style Your Form
Now let's add some styles to make our form look better.

```
body {
   font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
}

form {
   margin: 40px auto;
   max-width: 500px;
}

label {
   display: block;
   margin-top: 10px;
   font-weight: bold;
}

input[type=text], 
input[type=email], 
input[type=number] {  
   width: 100%; 
   padding: 12px;  
   border: 1px solid #ccc;  
   border-radius: 4px;  
   box-sizing: border-box;  
}

input[type=submit] {
   background-color: #007bff;
   color: white;
   padding: 12px 20px;
   border: none;
   border-radius: 4px;
   cursor: pointer;
}

#error_message {
   margin-top: 15px;
   color: red;
}
```

In this CSS stylesheet example, we've changed the font family of the body text and tweaked various aspects of the form styling such as label text styling, field dimensions, button colors and message container colors.

Step 3: Add JavaScript Validation and Error Handling
Finally, let's use jQuery to validate each form field before submission and show any errors in our error message container.

```
$(document).ready(function() {
   $('#myForm').on('submit', function(e) {
      e.preventDefault(); // Prevent default form submission

      // Validate Name Field
      var name = $('input[name=name]').val();
      if (!name) {
         showError('Please enter your name.');
         return false;
      } else if (!isValidName(name)) {
         showError('Invalid name format. Please use only letters and spaces.');
         return false;
      }

      // Validate Email Field
      var email = $('input[name=email]').val();
      if (!email) {
         showError('Please enter your email address.');
         return false;
      } else if (!isValidEmail(email)) {
         showError('Invalid email format.');
         return false;
      }

      // Validate Phone Number Field
      var phoneNumber = $('input[name=phone_number]').val();
      if (!phoneNumber ||!isValidPhoneNumber(phoneNumber)) {
         showError('Please enter a valid phone number.');
         return false;
      }

      // All validations passed, clear the error message and submit the form
      $('#error_message').empty();
      $(this).unbind('submit'); // Unbind event handler so it doesn't run again on form resubmission
      this.submit(); // Submit the form
   });

   function isValidName(name) {
      // Write your name validation logic here to ensure name does not contain numbers or special characters.
      var regex = /^[a-zA-Z ]+$/;
      return regex.test(name);
   }

   function isValidEmail(email) {
      // Write your email validation logic here to ensure email is in correct format ex...example@email.com
      var regex = /^[^\s@]+@[^\s@]+\.[^\s@]+$/;
      return regex.test(email);
   }

