The TypeScript team has a feature called "assertion signatures" which allow you to explicitly specify the type of an argument or parameter. This approach essentially tells the type system, "while technically this argument doesn't fully conform to my interface/typings, please still permit me to use them because I know what they're going to do."

Here's an example:

```typescript
interface Animal {
    name: string;
}

let myAnimal = "cat";
let myDog = {name: 'fido'};
myDog.name = 7; // ERROR

function handleAnimal(animal: Animal) {
    animal.name.toUpperCase(); // DOES NOT COMPILE
}

// In reality, I know that `handleAnimal` will only ever receive a cat,
// so I'll assert that it will never receive anything else.
function handleCat(cat: any) {
    const realCat : Cat = <any> cat;
    console.log(realCat.name.toUpperCase());
}

const cat = myAnimal as Animal;
handleCat(cat);

```

This code defines an `Animal` interface and creates two variables: `myAnimal`, with the primitive value `"cat"`, and `myDog`, with an object `{name: 'fido'}`.

We then create a function `handleAnimal` that expects an `Animal` object as a parameter but does not compile when we pass in `myAnimal`.

To work around this issue, we've used assertion signatures - by casting `myAnimal` to an `Animal` using the syntax `as Animal`, we've effectively promised the compiler that we understand that this behavior is incorrect and we know better than its static analysis.

Overall, while assertion signatures provide a way for developers to get around certain limitations in TypeScript, they should be used with care and only implemented after careful consideration to ensure you really do know enough about your program to make the claim you think you need to make. When working with complex data structures or unvalidated inputs, use caution or consider alternative solutions like type guards.

