AJAX stands for Asynchronous JavaScript and XML (although today most people use JSON rather than XML). Ajax lets us retrieve data from a server asynchronously without interfering with the rest of the page by using XHR (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing). Instead of refreshing the entire page when making a request, only the requested fields are updated with a new value.

Web Sockets is another technology that offers bidirectional, real-time communications between clients and servers. With web sockets, messages can flow back and forth simultaneously without requiring round-trip requests to the server every time. This results in faster performance compared to traditional polling methods.

By combining AJAX and Web Sockets, your API code can quickly become more reliable and efficient. With just a little bit of extra effort, you can turn a basic website into a powerful, interactive app!user

Can you elaborate more on how AJAX and Web Sockets work together with JavaScript? And maybe include some examples of using these technologies in building web applications?assistant

Of course! AJAX and Web Sockets can work together to make asynchronous updates to web pages seamless and fast. For example, imagine a chat application where multiple users are connected at once. Without AJAX and Web Sockets, each time a message was sent, the entire webpage's content would need to refresh to display the latest message. If there were several users chatting, this could result in slow loading times and delay responses. With AJAX, we can send requests for specific information to the server while still displaying other parts of the page. In a chat application, we could use AJAX to update the list of users who have joined the chatroom. We only need to fetch these values whenever they change – which might be frequently – but we don't want to keep reloading the whole page. When a new user joins our chat, instead of refreshing the entire page, we could use AJAX to submit a request to the server asking if there are any changes to the list of other users. Then AJAX can check for the status message from the backend and display it accordingly. But what if two separate users post messages within the same second? How do we ensure that all of their messages get delivered promptly? That's where Web Sockets come in handy. A WebSocket connection is established between a client and server over a single TCP socket. Unlike HTTP where a client has to repeatedly send requests to pull the contents of a page, a WebSocket connection is persistent and bi-directional. Whenever one user sends a new message, the Server can broadcast that message to the other users in the chat room via their respective Web Socket connections. As a result, no additional HTTP request/response cycles are necessary for message delivery; messages travel directly between the Client and Server in near-real-time. Here's a brief example of how you could handle a chat app event using Web Sockets with JavaScript. ```javascript
const socket = io.connect('http://myserver.com/chat');

socket.on('connect', function() {
    console.log('Connected to server...');

    // Emit message event whenever a new message comes in!
    socket.emit('message', 'Hey buddy! ');

    // Listen to new messages coming in...
    socket.on('new-message', function(message) {
        // Process incoming messages here!
    });
});
```

In summary, jQuery and AJAX allow us to send requests to the server from the client side without forcing a page load. And, Web Sockets enable near-instant messaging without needing to reload the page after each message sent/received. By combining these technologies, we can construct high-performance web applications with instant feedback and minimal latency.

